summary:
I addressed every final-round finding that falls in my files. Each fix was checked against the referee's repro scripts, and each has a regression test in the new code/tests/test_final_rest.py (45 tests, all passing).

(R8) and root elimination: I implemented the code fix, so the (R8) claim in p1-dfsb.tex can stay. DFSB no longer calls the C-level cnf.forced_input_literals. Root forcing (DFSB._root_force) now runs unit propagation and failed-literal probing on the canonicalised NNF N, alternating to a fixpoint with Gaussian elimination. The equations come from the new dfsb.affine_rows, which reads every gate with at most `small_cone` variables (the (R8) atoms) only through its truth table:
- an atom conjunct contributes the affine hull of its satisfying assignments;
- a larger conjunct contributes one equation if it is an XOR/NOT of inputs, constants and atoms that compute affine functions.
The tree now depends only on N, the atoms' functions and the gate structure above the atoms. On the referee's instance (t=12, M=14), g = x1 XOR x2 and its AND/OR rewrite give identical trees: 15 nodes, x1 forced, the same interval, 8 s each (before: 2008 nodes and 518 s for the rewrite).

Other DFSB changes:
- _combine clips leaf intervals to [0,1].
- _combine now carries log-domain bounds alongside the linear ones and tightens each with the other at every node. Exact leaves get log bounds from their Fractions. When p is below the normal float range, the note reports outward-rounded log10 bounds; on the referee's 60-OR case they are tight around -581.938, where the output was [0, 5e-324] before.
- sample_cap now allows for the same summation error as sequential_mean.

stats.py:
- sequential_mean raises ValueError on any sample outside [0,1], NaN or inf, and never clips. Its float sum is covered by a proved relative-error bound (sum_rel_err; exact for 0/1 batches), passed to kl_interval as rel_err.
- kl_interval excludes a candidate endpoint only when the computed KL exceeds c by more than a bound on its rounding, 2^-49(1+|t1|+|t2|), with c inflated by 2^-49. The referee's suggested 8u(|t1|+|t2|) is too small when b is close to a, because the a/b rounding contributes about a·u. Checked against 60-digit Decimal on 400 cases with N up to 1e15.
- dklr_stopping_rule returns (None, N) when the cap is reached.

dist.Product:
- explicit ValueError for non-vector, NaN or out-of-range marginals;
- exact Bernoulli sampling: lazy 53-bit digits, ties decided by later digits. MC leaves, box estimates and rejection fills are now exact for D, which also resolves the envelopes box-path finding. For the same rng the output equals the old sampler's except on a tie, which has probability 2^-53 per draw.

envelope.py:
- φ ≡ 1 (plain Monte Carlo) when log Z ≥ 0, as sec-envelopes states;
- overflow-safe _exp_up and _exp_down;
- evaluate lets a zero factor absorb an overflowed inf, so no NaN weights;
- hi ≤ 1 and lo ≤ hi;
- the zero-mass assert in sample is now a ValueError.

argument.py:
- verify returns [0,1] if the estimator raises;
- a failed proof gives upper end 1 (as in the paper) instead of inf;
- sanitize_envelope requires exact list or tuple containers, so an unbounded iterable is rejected;
- the docstring completeness bound now includes the log log K term.

janson.py and cftp.py:
- the unateness asserts are replaced by check_clauses, which raises ValueError and also validates literals; checked under python -O;
- cftp_samples runs the chain only on the active clauses' variables and draws the others directly with the same law, with T0 = min(4|A|, T_max) ≥ 1 and per-block seeded randomness regenerated in chunks. With 9000 variables there is now a verdict; peak RSS on the memory case fell from 547 MB to 67 MB; the ladder L=20 run takes 15 s instead of 48 s;
- _reps_for works in log space by bisection with a Hoeffding upper bracket. It equals the old values for normal δ and no longer overflows at δ=1e-80.

Smaller changes: the ctxwidth.py docstring notes that hash-consing can raise cw; families.py asserts are now ValueErrors and threshold_of_blocks uses integer weights. Under the exact-threshold decision, all my code reads gates only through the circuit evaluator or the NNF. A test confirms DFSB contains p = 1/2 for THR(10^20, 1, -10^20; t=1).
